  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Cole held serve through seven, and they scratched out enough to hold on by one.",
    "what_this_means": "They led from the second inning and never trailed until Atlanta tied it in the fifth. That's when everything got tight. Cole had given them clean innings and a manageable pace. Six hits through seven. But the margin evaporated fast. The offense had done its part in small doses: Lombard went deep, Jones drove in two, and they held firm at 5-4. The bullpen closed it out cleanly. Bednar got the save, but this was about holding what they had, not building on it. A one-run win feels routine until you realize it's the second in a row at this margin. They're not winning because anything is breaking. They're winning because nothing is breaking, and that's a narrower path than this division allows.",
    "what_to_watch": "Grant Holmes starts for Atlanta at 1:35 PM tonight with an ERA sitting at 3.67. Basically Cole's mirror. The rematch will depend on whether the Yankees can do more than scratch and survive against a pitcher they already saw go deep.",
